  "explanation": {
    "short": "A dot (ं) or a moon-with-dot (ँ) above a letter adds a soft \"n\" through the nose: मैं, हाँ, नहीं all end in a nasal sound, said through the nose, with the mouth open. Before another letter the dot says the nasal that belongs to it: हिंदी is hin-dī.",
    "more": "The two marks sound almost the same. ँ is usual on a long ā at the end of a word (हाँ, कहाँ, पाँच), and ं on most other words. The nasal sound is soft: you do not close your mouth on it."
  },
